1. What is Set in Java?
Set is a collection interface in java.util that represents a collection of unique elements.

Key Characteristics:
❌ No duplicates
❌ No index-based access
Order depends on implementation
Allows at most one null (implementation-dependent)
Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

Interview one-liner:
“Set is a collection that does not allow duplicate elements.”

3. Common Set Implementations
| Implementation | Ordering        | Data Structure           | Thread-Safe | Best Use           |
| -------------- | --------------- | ------------------------ | ----------- | ------------------ |
| HashSet        | No order        | Hash table               | ❌           | Fast lookup        |
| LinkedHashSet  | Insertion order | Hash table + linked list | ❌           | Order + uniqueness |
| TreeSet        | Sorted          | Red-Black Tree           | ❌           | Sorted data        |

4. HashSet (🔥 Most Used)
Example
  import java.util.*;
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();
          set.add("Java");
          set.add("Spring");
          set.add("Java");
  
          System.out.println(set);
      }
  }
  
  Output (order not guaranteed)
  [Java, Spring]

Key Points:
Duplicate ignored
O(1) average for add/remove/search
Allows one null

5. LinkedHashSet (Insertion Order Preserved)
Example
  import java.util.*;
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          Set<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();
          set.add(30);
          set.add(10);
          set.add(20);
          set.add(10);
  
          System.out.println(set);
      }
  }
  
  Output
  [30, 10, 20]

6. TreeSet (Sorted Set)
Example
  import java.util.*;
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          Set<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();
          set.add(30);
          set.add(10);
          set.add(20);
  
          System.out.println(set);
      }
  }
  
  Output
  [10, 20, 30]

Key Points:
Sorted in natural order
❌ Does not allow null
O(log n) operations

